\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces We check possible sample size biases for Qwen3-30B-A3B, since MoE layers receive different numbers of tokens. We examine the correlation between the number of routed tokens per expert and the energy ratio $R@0.99$ and find none.}}{106}{table.caption.127}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Model architecture and training budgets. Dense-600M uses a narrower MLP; the other architecture settings are shared.}}{112}{table.caption.133}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Benchmark accuracy (\%) for the dense and sparse models. CORE reports the mean centered score (\%). BB denotes BIG-bench.}}{114}{table.caption.136}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Evaluation of Dense-1.2B (Dense) and ZoN-1.2B-A600M (Sparse). All entries are percentages; CORE is the mean centered score. $\%0$ measures the fraction of zeros predicted by ZoN MLP gates during inference. BB denotes BIG-bench.}}{115}{table.caption.138}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces Performance of NV-Embed representations on classification and retrieval tasks. Scores are percentages. $k$ is the number of coordinates retained at inference; dense representations use all $d_{\text {model}}$ coordinates. The dense baseline uses NV-Embed with a projector fine-tuned on each task and retains all coordinates. The best score in each column is bold and the second-highest distinct score is underlined.}}{116}{table.caption.140}%
